Yes. Opportunity cost is the value of the best alternative you give up when you choose something. And there’s a real distinction between hearing the term, understanding it, and noticing it while you still have a choice.

Say you spend three hours tweaking a Wild Hawg bag. The cost depends on what you would actually have done with those hours. If you would’ve filmed the first Beverage Goblin sketch, the cost is a chance to see whether people laugh. If you would’ve spent the evening with the boys, it’s that evening. The clock gives you the price in hours. The alternative tells you what you paid for it.

A few concepts work the same way. Each one makes a different question visible.

Concept	The question it gives you
Sunk cost	Given where I am today, would I spend the next hour or dollar on this?
Marginal returns	How much will one more pass improve it?
Value of information	What could I learn cheaply that would change my decision?
Option value	What future choices does this move keep open?
Compounding	If I keep doing this, what becomes easier or more valuable over time?
Status quo bias	If I were choosing from scratch today, would I pick my current setup?

Your point about what we tell ourselves fits directly. “I’m just polishing the bag” describes one action. “I’m spending the evening polishing the bag, and the sketch waits another week” describes the decision. You might still choose the bag. Now you can see both things you’re choosing between.